-names>K</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>1995</year><article-title>A specific neutrophil elastase inhibitor (ONO-5046.Na) attenuates LPS-induced acute lung inflammation in the hamster</article-title><source>Eur Respir J</source><volume>8</volume><fpage>1293</fpage><lpage>9</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7489793</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="f1-copd-2-283" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>(<bold>Upper</bold>) Mouse lung exposed to cigarette smoke for 3 months shows significant airspace enlargement. (<bold>Lower</bold>) Mouse lung treated with aerosolized HA during the same 3-month period of smoke exposure shows only minimal airspace enlargement.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="copd-2-283f1"/></fig><fig id="f2-copd-2-283" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>HA is a long-chain polysaccharide composed of repeating disaccharide units of glucuronic acid and n-acetylglucosamine. When administered intratracheally, it binds to alveolar septal elastic fibers and may prevent their degradation by elastases released from macrophages and neutrophils.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="copd-2-283f2"/></fig><fig id="f3-copd-2-283" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Photomicrograph showing attachment of fluorescein-labeled HA to alveolar macrophages. By coating the cell surface, HA could block interactions with various cytokines.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="copd-2-283f3"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>